you all probably know by now that when  channels like mine shows like mine talk  about stories like the one I'm about to  talk about we get demonetised  and penalized on YouTube and Facebook  but what has been captured on video over  the last 24 to 48 hours is two insane  and it's too important not to tell you  about it and to show it to you so let's  dig right into it first of all a New  York Police Department officers have  been caught on video  beating a cyclist with batons as he  tries to cross the street  the cyclist seems to be crossing his  bike while wearing a helmet at a  crosswalk mind you not even jaywalking  and multiple officers are just beating  the crap out of him and it looks like  they're beating the cyclist on the upper  leg eventually the cyclist is forced off  of his bike bike before the video ends  we don't know exactly what led up to it  but at this point in the video this is  not an individual that is resisting and  there are multiple officers beating him  with batons and this led to  congresswoman Alexandria Casio Cortes to  tweet quote horrifying you start with  the tiny possibility that two officers  may be walking over to de-escalate the  abusive one instead they join in beating  a man trying to get out of traffic this  isn't a problem of bad apples or  incidents this is an institutional and  systemic crisis and the reason I'm  picking this incident to talk about is  that congresswoman AOC s response  mirrors exactly what I've been talking  about all week when she says this isn't  a problem of bad apples or incidents  this is an institutional and systemic  crisis when I did my first commentary  about the protests and the civil unrest  that we're experiencing on the Monday  show that was the main point that I  tried to make the there were really two  points point one was anybody claiming  some grand unifying theory about this  this is all about capitalism this is all  about race this is all about police  brutality it's not so any grand unifying  theory that focuses on on one element is  destined to be to fall short but maybe  most  that the individual incidents may give  us a window into the systemic problems a  lens a suggestion but really we need to  be talking about the systemic aspects  that are plaguing our country and the  debate over good and bad police doesn't  actually address the systemic issues  that are at the heart of much of what is  going on in another video from North  Carolina police likewise were filmed in  this video they are destroying elements  of medical for lack of a better term a  sort of medical station that had some  medics and basic first-aid supplies and  a lot of bottled water you can see in  this video police standing above a ton  of destroyed and poured out bottled  water there in Asheville North Carolina  a report said that police hit some of  the medics with their shields and  knocked people to the ground and this is  again you know we can talk about  individuals but there's something  particularly nefarious in this instance  because of the principle called medical  neutrality so um we have norms that are  being ignored we've talked about you  don't go after press and we've seen  press attacked both by some protesters  and by police in different places over  the last week but now we're talking  about the principle of medical  neutrality in any conflict you do not go  after anybody who is providing medical  services you have a protest you have  police most protesters are peaceful most  police are good there's some scuffle you  do not pull the medical people into it  police may try to argue well you know if  the medical people weren't there the  protesters would be less brazen and  aggressive no at the international level  going after the the medical going after  medical is a violation of the Geneva  Convention these are of course domestic  protests we're talking about domestic  law enforcement the Geneva Conventions  don't apply but if this conflict instead  of being between protesters and looters  or police and protesters or police and  looters or whatever if this were a  conflict between two countries and the  Geneva Convention rules applied  either side destroying medical elements  would be a violation of the Geneva  Convention so we've got attacks on media  people from the press hit with rubber  bullets sprayed hit with shields almost  everything we've seen over the last  seven days the point of this segment is  not let's pick on police or particular  police any more than I pick on  individual protesters most protesters  are peaceful protesters are peaceful the  ones that aren't peaceful are really not  protesters they're doing other things  they're looters they're instigators  insiders whatever provocateurs police  are mostly doing the right thing and we  have shows of support and kneeling and  and singing and everything with police  from all over the country but this is  about the bigger picture we have a  horrible situation going on much of it  because of protests because of police  brutality and we are seeing everything  from cyclists beaten to a medical  station destroyed to a president saying  hey here's an idea for how to solve the  protests and unrest caused by police  brutality let's encourage more brutality  against protesters everything we are  seeing at a systemic level is the  opposite of what we need thankfully at  the individual level many police  departments have been great many  individual officers have been great  we've seen protesters bringing water to  police we've seen police joining  protesters and all of this stuff but  unless the systems are changed nothing  will change and so later on today a lot  of people on my streams this week and  via email have been saying David can you  explain how to deal with police  brutality and essentially none of it has  to do with the individual it is all  systemic changes and I'm going to  outline them for you a little bit later  on in the program
 

